{By Raphael, who bound the demon and opened the eyes of the blind, I call the mender forth. | In the name of him that spake and it was done, I begin, and I do not begin twice. | I call thee, healer, thou that standest at the bedside of kings. Stand at this bedside now.} {The pure art, stripped of ornament: force, bounded and directed. | They call it the common art. They have never stood in its path. | Raw power is shaped by sign and word, and it strikes exactly where it is sent.} {Word upon word, and no breath taken between, for a breath is a door and I have shut every door. | If thou delayest, I curse thee into the lake of fire, and strike thy name from every book. | Answer in the first hour or answer in the second. Thou wilt answer, for the circle does not tire.}

{Let my wisdom be the hand and thy strength the arm, and let the work be done between us. | By the water, by the oil, by the clean cloth: the injury is cast out and forbidden to return. | Thou art called, thou art come, thou art bound. Say after me that thou art bound.} {Something is commanded to leave the body. | It is done because it was said, and said in the order the old book sets down. | The compulsion holds as long as the breath that carried it, and not one moment longer.}

{Speak nothing of the mending for three days, counting from the dawn that follows. | The chalk is washed away with running water, and the water poured where no one walks. | The book is closed upon the page, and the page is not marked.}

793:
{By fire, and by the iron in the fire, I call thee out against every enemy. | Adonai, Elohim, and the name after them which no mouth may speak: be present at this working. | Come forth, come forth, come forth, out of the abyss and into the figure I have drawn for thee.} {Raw power is shaped by sign and word, and it strikes exactly where it is sent. | They call it the common art. They have never stood in its path. | The whole of it is mass and speed, arriving together at the one point it was aimed.} {By him that liveth for ever, whose name is a tower: do the thing that I have said. | I set the sign upon every enemy, and I do not take it off. | Whom I name, thou knowest. What I ask, thou canst. What I forbid, thou wilt not attempt.}

{I have thy seal in my hand. I close my hand. Know what that is. | By the ring of Solomon upon my finger, kneel, and do as thou wast made to do. | I bind thee by thy name, by thine office, by the legions under thee, and by the hour that is passing.} {The blow falls from a thing that was told to strike, and it does not hesitate. | Power of Arcanism is borrowed from below, and what is borrowed is paid back in breath. | The working feeds on the operator's intellect, drawn off without mercy.}

{Let the lamp burn out of itself. To blow it out is to speak once more, and once more is once too many. | The circle is swept at {city}, and the sweepings are given to running water. | The masters of {faction} keep the long dismissal, and speak it to the last word.}

855:
{Q = mc dT. Hellfire is written whole in that line, and the caster is the term that moves. | dS >= 0. The law has never once been broken, and Hellfire only chooses where the increase falls. | Every engine is a fall from hot to cold. Hellfire is the engine, and the fall is steep.} {The flame is fed, the flame is raised, and the flame is loosed upon the field. | First the smoke, then the heat, then the light, and the order is never reversed. | Everything it touches is changed in one direction, and there is no road back.} {As above, so below, in heat as in all things, and neither is asked. | Vent it or it bursts. Everything I touch here would rather burst. | The fire goes where the cold is, and I move the cold.}

{Draw the gradient tight as a bowstring, then release it in one stroke. | The cycle has four strokes, and in two of them the practitioner is the working fluid. | Temperature is read at three points. If the middle point disagrees, the working is ended at once.} {Heat goes from warm to cold and asks nothing. The art is deciding which is which. | Every fire is a fall. I only choose where it lands. | A difference is made, and every difference is a debt against the world.}

{In {nation} the working is named in law and forbidden by name. | Tabulated values assume standard conditions. The field has never once supplied them. | The working hand loses its feeling first. It is counted as the price of the craft.}

934:
{Cleared for utterance. Say what is printed. What was struck out, say inwardly. | Every mind is a locked room. I am the one who was given the key, and then erased. | PSI Lockdown. Spoken once. Indoors. By one voice. Never again by the same mouth.} {Nothing here can be weighed. Will is the only blade. | One mind closes its grip on another, and neither grip is made of flesh. | The battle is fought inside a mind, and the mind is not always the caster's.} {Repeat the phrase eleven times. The true count is [REDACTED]. Eleven is enough to kill. | The mantra is only the fence the working walks along. | The thought grows heavier than iron, and I do not set it down.}

{Do not want it. Wanting is noise. Noise ends the working and sometimes the worker. | I hold the certainty that one enemy is already beaten, and I hold it until it spreads. | I bind by clearance: whatever stands above my level, come down to it.} {The All is Mind. The rest of the sentence is above any mortal clearance. | It works on anything with a mind, and the dark has always had one. | Nothing crosses the space between, for there was never any space between.}

{PSI Lockdown is ended. What it touched is not. | Do not look at the target afterwards. Looking is what opens it again. | Come back. Take the name up again. Put it on carefully; it has been lying on the floor.}

935:
